{"kind":"assert","text":"Make request inspection show renewed commitments\n\nRecommendation for Hugh: make a request's inspection expose its commitment history, with the current or completed delivery easy to find. Preserve exact promise identity. This removes a misleading read surface without adding a workflow state or asking authors to rewrite history.\n\nA concrete example is novice-CLI request #15538. Live MCP inspection of the request returns only its first promise, marked `reneged`, and an empty landing object. Inspecting its renewed promise instead returns `satisfied`, the approved candidate, and sealed receipt 7db3b347; the service also measures that landing as incorporated locally and in origin/main. I independently verified the approved candidate is an ancestor of current main. The work was delivered and must not be revived. At verified depth 19830, 27 requests have multiple commitment rows; two have this first-withdrawn, later-satisfied pattern. Both request inspections reproduce it. See the [frozen evidence and controls](inspection-lifecycle-evidence.json).\n\nThe [shared inspection builder](internal/statusview/query.go@33f69956167115d7cc3235b6b1864ed904f2802a:521) stops at the first matching commitment. It also builds related evidence from that selected chain. The [CLI renderer](cmd/gs/query.go@33f69956167115d7cc3235b6b1864ed904f2802a:313) presents the resulting single status without disclosing other promises. These source files are byte-identical at their live publication, the running reader, and current main. This is a verified MCP/shared-inspection finding; I have not established that the browser makes the same selection.\n\nThe smallest useful repair belongs in shared inspection: for a request, disclose the matching lifecycle rows in a bounded, deterministic list with an explicit omitted count and exact promise/report/receipt links. Make the relevant current or completed lifecycle readily visible; show concurrent ambiguity explicitly rather than choosing one by position. An inspection of a particular promise or report must continue to show that exact lifecycle. Keep ordinary staleness beside its lifecycle status. Do not let a convenience summary choose authority for review or merge.\n\nAcceptance should use one withdrawn promise followed by a renewed promise and sealed delivery: inspecting the request exposes the delivery and the earlier withdrawal; inspecting either promise still gives its own outcome. Add a simultaneous-live-lifecycles control and a bounded-history control. Run the same examples through CLI and MCP, then check the browser for the same assumption before expanding UI scope.\n\nThis is a recommendation for review, not an implementation assignment. It complements #19081's usability findings and is separate from #19651's exact-lifecycle authorization correction and #19659's stale-attention policy work.\n","body":{"head":"33f69956167115d7cc3235b6b1864ed904f2802a","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:43be4c094939c8d8cff52a2741fa7776463b8be2, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e8c2069b481a1aecf513e5701d49afb29d4d6482 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:10e5cfd1aafbe57878b264ea369e5ce099c36f43, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:429f56c3a259e28dd4ccbe3112abec7e8e475caa, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:57b50151f90f2abe8c0df1f518939917f214c10d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5e6fb9019f41c51de2bc354a7fb8526f5afe2bfd and 2 more"}}